                        "review": "It'd be quite a novel concept circa 2015-2017 but nowadays it pales in comparison with anything like it. Lo-fi art is ok (even though it wouldn't hurt to make it more consistent), music/SFX are serviceable, I haven't noticed many crashes or game-breaking bugs.\n\nNow, the main problem with this game lies in the domain of game design. There's multiple problems that make it obnoxious instead of fun and overly simplistic instead of having engaging systems. It's repetitive and boring, there's very little content besides grinding achievements which are not that numerous and pretty easy to get once you understand the right strategy.\n\nIt's also not that the game has a minimalistic approach to its content since there's a plethora of examples of games which the their innate minimalism did not stop from being fun or engaging.\n\nThere's more issues, but those are the most annoying: \n[list]\n    [*] The darkness in this game borders on game-breaking. I understand what the developer intended by it, but instead of pressuring it's just outright annoying. IDK what it is, the radius, the fading time, the minimum radius, but it's evidently not fine-tuned. It reminded me of the dark lava level from Sega Genesis Ghostbusters (1990) which, if you played the game, you know the magnitude of frustration.\n    [*] To top the almost ever-present darkness off, the traps are a royal clusterfuck. You have to either memorize the whole map with every trap's exact position or be very careful when traversing, which is impossible given that you are always running out of either health, ammo or batteries. The whole debacle would be easily solved with an audio cue when approaching a trap, but alas, we get crushed by it yet again.\n    [*] The pick-ups can spawn INSIDE of a trap, even a static one, which makes it unavailable for the rest of the session.\n    [*] The zombies will run to the very same position the player is located at, and given there's no collision, they can just walk inside of you. It's bad not only visually, but it means once that happened, you won't be able to kill that zombie unless you move, since bullets spawn a little bit further away from the center of the player.\n   [*] Just any variety, really. Adding just a few more levels, a few types of zombies, meta-progression in form of upgrading the character, guns or pickups would drastically increase re-playability, but there's none. Zero. Nada.\n[/list]\n\nThis game could have potential, but in present form, I can't recommend it to anyone. It's cheap all right, especially on discount, so that's at least some silver lining.",
